A||||12||AkursedX||Autos vs Manuals at the track.||||1||
Z||000000||AkursedX||08-30-2001||09:55 AM||||Ok, this has really been irking me lately.  Here on this forum and amongst my friends, there is always talk about how manuals are better than automatics at the track, and people reall put down autos.  <P>Don't get me wrong, yes I know the manuals have better acceleration and gearing and whatnot, so I can see why stock for stock, the manual is better.  <P>But when people are talking about adding gobs of horsepower, there are alot more problems for a manual to face.  The #1 problem being the clutch slipping.  Whereas an automatic can be built up to handle that kind of power more effieciently.  Plus you don't have to worry about missing a shift, and if you get a high stall converter, then you are definietly running at least even, and the auto would have more consistant runs.<P>Also, if you look at top fuel dragsters and things similar to that, I think they are almost exclusively automatics.  There are Buicks Grand Nationals run 8's and they are autos.  The only Toyota Supra I know of in the 8's is an auto.  <P>So I guess what I'm saying is that if you are looking for a real high-powered car that you want to mainly use for the strip, I think the auto would be the way to go.  But if you are just looking to mildly modify your car, then the manual will probably be better.<P>I'd like to hear some other people's opinions on this.  And I didn't mean to offend anyone who owns a manual.  I own two automatic Fieros, and they serve me great since most of my driving is in the city.  But I would like to have a 5-speed to autocross one day.  <P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||DecadenceR||08-30-2001||10:22 AM||||From what I've read and heard, autos are able to handle more HP because of the way the gears grab. YOu have full contact with the auto transmission, where as the manual only has 3 teeth that engage and at higher horsepower it's really easy to damage that. but like you said, for stock or slight mods, manual is far better for driver-control.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||08-30-2001||11:28 AM||||Also depends on type of track you're driving. I think MHO that a road course is better suited for a manual, unless you want to manually shift an automatic.<P>Drag racing is better left for an automatic, unless you know how to shift.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||08-30-2001||04:12 PM||||Get yourself a good 'ole train lever in the centre console. Gotta love slap-shift   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Auto isn't a bad thing. I the Fiero's case, the problem is more with the gearing. My auto is about as fast as the 4-spd, at least for a while. However, because my gears are short, it starts getting hard to keep up. I'm looking into a higher stall, for sure, and investigating a stage-II shifty-kit. Not much I can do with the gearing, so I'll have to compensate with HP  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000004||chester||08-30-2001||04:40 PM||||I have run into this debate a lot as well. In drag racing I feel that an auto IS the way to go.  Like you said, no worry about missed gears and lets face it, that auto can shift faster then 99.9% of us out there. Sticks are fun to drive and "sporty". I have another 86 GT that I'm debating on stripping out and turning it into a drag car keeping it barely street legal.  Auto and V8 is the way I will go. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||1
Z||000005||Neal||08-30-2001||06:33 PM||||i was recently talking with an old drag racer, when he was racing he used a manual (340 Duster running mid to high 10's).<P>Now in his toy car (early 80's buick wagon, 455cubes, BIGGEST sleeper ive ever seen) he is going to install an auto with manual valve bodies. Meaning auto gear box and shifting BUT it wont shift untill you shift the lever.<P>But for a road course a manual is ALWAYS better||||reg||1
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||08-30-2001||08:49 PM||||AkursedX:<P>I have seen some Killer autocross cars that were auto's. But they all had gobs of torque , ie small blocks , 4.3ltr v6's. Most autocrosses don't require more than first gear, you would need to manually shift the auto. I'm not too sure what sorta speed you can get out of an auto in 1st gear , mph wise , but it can be done. I find manual fiero's run out of power in 1st gear , not only mph , but HP. The motor falls flat on it's face. This is fine with a TPi vette that has the same sort of torque curve becuase it can turn 56mph in first gear , but with a fiero we seem to run out of power in 1st , and 2nd gear seems to have gear spacing that is less desirable<P><BR>My suggestion to you for autocrossing your car. Toss the 17's for autocrossing, get a set of 14'-15's with low pro tires. You can buy used 14"- 15" rims cheap , I have sold 2 sets GT lace wheels for $125 candian. I would get 225/50/15's rear in a R1 style tire and 215/50's front. This gives you a tire that is fairly short , ie the car will pull harder but run out of breath earlier. This is a toss up again , you can hold the car in 2nd gear and even with your 3.33(35?) gears you will be able to have enough pull to get out of situations.<P>I ran Comp ta R1's , 225/50/15's all around on my car 3 years ago , the tire was 22.7" tall , the car sat lower than it does now. It would pull like a freight train through every gear.<P>17's are more stable , and usually the same height as stock 60 series rubber. Mine are within a  few 10ths of an inch of stock height.<P>Just ordered some slicks for the back<BR>Bought a used set of Michilen Pilot sports (17") and a few hours ago got a set of 25% worn Pirelli P Zero's froma  local porsche racer for free..<P>lots to burn on the track , at the strip , and on the street.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000007||ray b||08-30-2001||09:09 PM||||f-1 cars used autos to roadrace till baned<BR>now their back in<BR>jim hall used a auto in can-am<BR>mopars SS/a auto beat the sticks in the 1/4<BR>new GM little caddy will have a 5spd auto soon and maybe a 6 speed stick[auto-week]<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||08-31-2001||03:08 AM||||If we are talking about a purpose built dragster, than yes, an auto will usually be faster for two reasons, first has already been stated (speed of shifts, consistency)<BR>second:<P>The torque converter is just that, stick the engine at peak torque NOW, and let the speed change to suit.   <P>****IF**** (and this is a big if  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> you have enough horsepower to overcome all driveline losses, then this is actually a better (and smoother, and broader) accelerative curve than a manual.<P>You must essentially have unlimited(or at least insane, read above 500) BHP for this to work however.<P>The perfect transmission for drag racing is the (as yet low-power only) CVT.<P>If you're going to be turning, IMHO a manual is hands-down better all the time.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>f-1 cars used autos to roadrace till baned<BR>now their back in<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh, no.  If you think F1 trannys have anything to do at all with the tranny in the next caddy that zigs, well, uh.  Then you have built a functional Space Shuttle in your backyard using toothpicks and glue.<P>Those F1 trannys are electronically controled manual gearboxes. (no torque converters, at least, not in any I am familiar with..)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||pontiacs4me||08-31-2001||03:48 AM||||do companies make higher stall converters for a 4t60 tranny?  Or even a 125?  I havent ever seen any advertised, but maybe someone can take a stock converter and raise the stall on it, is this possible?||||reg||1
Z||000010||ray b||08-31-2001||12:23 PM||||yes in F-1 clutch is used but shift is automatic computer controled by paddle on stearing wheel<BR>jim hall used a tork converter in road racer<BR>won some tooo.<BR>no the new caddy ain't F-1 but is GM and may bolt up to our cars and i'd like a 5 speed auto or a 6 speed stick at a junk yard price.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000011||KissMySSFiero||08-31-2001||02:43 PM||||what about a lanko(sp)  thats what all the big money drag cars use.  its a manual that only uses the clutch on take off.  then you just pull a lever when its time to shift.  if you ever watch the inside view of a drag car the guy will have several shifters to pull down.  you cant miss the shift and you cant put it in the wrong gear.  and no clutches.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bazooka||08-31-2001||04:07 PM||||*****************************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>do companies make higher stall converters for a 4t60 tranny?  Or even a 125?  I havent ever seen any advertised, but maybe someone can take a stock converter and raise the stall on it, is this possible?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>******************************************<BR>I have a 3400 rpm stallspeed in my 125c Fiero Trans. Roadmasters Transmissions has em. <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <BR>******************************************<BR>||||reg||1
